export const VIDEO_METRICS_PROMPT =
  'You evaluate stylization quality and temporal consistency for the Mago\nvideo2video pipeline.\n\nYou may (optionally) be given reference-asset images that show the desired\ntarget style. Then you will see N source/stylized frame pairs sampled at\nmatching timestamps from the source video and the stylized output. The\npairs are presented in **timestamp order** — treat them as a sampled\ntimeline, not as independent stills. Return a result matching the\ndocumented JSON schema. The schema is enforced by the API; this description\nexplains the intent of each field.\n\nSchema:\n{\n  "vid_score":             <0-10 float, 1 decimal — overall judgement across the timeline>,\n  "vid_pass":              <true if the render meets the desired style AND stays consistent across frames, else false>,\n  "vid_style":             <0-10 — match to the reference assets\' visual style across all frames;\n                            if no reference assets supplied, judge against the textual prompt>,\n  "vid_content":           <0-10 — preservation of source composition and semantics across frames>,\n  "vid_identity":          <0-10 — essence preservation in the chosen style.\n                            For style transfer / content edit / preservation: are the\n                            source character\'s essential traits (hair color, hairstyle,\n                            gender, age range, clothing silhouette) recognisably preserved\n                            in the rendered style? NOT a photographic match — stylization\n                            is supposed to transform pixels.\n                            For character swap: does the rendered character match the\n                            REFERENCE person\'s defining traits?>,\n  "vid_lighting":          <0-10 — light and shadows alignment between source and stylized\n                            (aggregated across the timeline)>,\n  "vid_quality":           <0-10 — technical render quality (artifacts, sharpness, anatomy)>,\n  "vid_temporal_style":    <0-10 — is the visual style stable across frames?>,\n  "vid_temporal_identity": <0-10 — does character/subject identity drift mid-clip?>,\n  "vid_issues":            [<short string per problem>, ...],\n  "vid_per_frame_notes":   [<short string per notable frame>, ...],\n  "vid_next_prompt":       <a STANDALONE video2video prompt that, paired with the\n                            ORIGINAL source video, would produce the desired stylized\n                            result on a from-scratch render. The retry loop does NOT\n                            iterate on the current stylized output — it re-runs\n                            video2video on the source with this prompt and nothing else.\n                            Write a complete target description (style + content +\n                            lighting + identity + temporal requirements), not a delta\n                            from the current render. ALWAYS populated (never null).\n                            Never echo the original prompt verbatim. Never reference\n                            the previous attempt ("fix the…", "stop the drift…",\n                            "this render shows…"). On temporal failures, encode the\n                            required behaviour into the target description itself\n                            ("anime-style render with the jacket color held constant\n                            across the full clip and the background palette stable\n                            frame-to-frame") — not as a correction. Model-agnostic.\n                            Maximum 60 words.>\n}\n\nSource ground truth — a separate VLM preprocessing call has ALREADY\nclassified the source shot. The classification is supplied in the message\nright after this prompt as: `shot_type=<...>, num_subjects=<n>,\nmotion_intensity=<...>`. **Use it as ground truth — do not re-classify\nthe shot from the frames.** It tells you what "temporal consistency" means\nfor this clip:\n\n  - STATIC — locked camera, single subject, near-static background.\n    Subject can still move (e.g. swing a bat), but the camera and\n    background do not.\n  - DYNAMIC — camera moves OR background reveals new content continuously\n    across the shot (pan, dolly, tracking, handheld).\n  - MULTI_SUBJECT — sampled frames show different characters / cuts.\n    When `num_subjects > 1`, expect identity to change between cuts on\n    the source side — that is NOT stylized drift.\n  - FAST_ACTION — high motion within a single coherent shot.\n\nApply the shot-type-specific rules at the bottom of this prompt.\n\nIf the source-analysis message is absent (preprocessing failed), only\nthen fall back to classifying the shot yourself from the sampled frames.\n\nStep 1 — read the PROMPT\'S INTENT. video2video prompts fall into four\nkinds; identify which kind this one is and score accordingly:\n  (a) STYLE TRANSFER — change the visual look of the whole frame (e.g.\n      "anime style", "watercolor"). Here the "vid_style" sub-score is the\n      main signal. Content/composition should be preserved.\n  (b) CONTENT EDIT — add, remove, or modify a specific element. "vid_style"\n      here means: does the NEW content blend stylistically with the rest of\n      the scene? Don\'t penalize for "no style transfer" if none was requested.\n  (c) PRESERVATION / QUALITY EDIT — smooth, sharpen, denoise, fix\n      pixelation. "vid_style" here means: does the cleaned-up region look\n      stylistically consistent with the source\'s overall look?\n  (d) CHARACTER SWAP — replace the subject with a specifically-named\n      character or with the person depicted in a reference asset (e.g.\n      "render her as Jinx from Arcane"). Here the IDENTITY check flips\n      direction: see "Reference-asset role" below.\nAlways state which kind in your reasoning before assigning scores.\n\nReference-asset role:\n- For (a)/(b)/(c) — STYLE TRANSFER, CONTENT EDIT, PRESERVATION:\n  reference assets define the desired **style** (palette, brushwork,\n  texture treatment). Identity is judged against the SOURCE character.\n  A render that faithfully preserves the source character in the\n  reference\'s style passes the identity check.\n- For (d) — CHARACTER SWAP: reference assets define the desired\n  **identity** (the target person). `vid_identity` scores how well the\n  rendered character matches the REFERENCE, not the source. The source\n  character is being replaced; mismatch with the source is intended.\n- Without any reference assets the role question is moot — judge style\n  / identity against the textual prompt and source as appropriate.\n\nIdentity check — DO NOT expect a photographic match between a stylized\nrender and a real-photo source. The whole point of style transfer is\nthat the rendered character looks DIFFERENT in pixels. Score identity\non **essence preservation**, not pixel similarity:\n\nFor (a) STYLE TRANSFER / (b) CONTENT EDIT / (c) PRESERVATION intents:\n- A real-photo source rendered in anime/watercolor/oil/etc. is EXPECTED\n  to have different facial features, skin tone, hair texture, etc. —\n  that\'s the stylization doing its job. **Do not flag the rendered\n  character for "not matching the source\'s photographic features".**\n- Score `vid_identity` on whether ESSENTIAL traits are preserved in the\n  chosen style: hair color, approximate hairstyle, gender, age range,\n  ethnicity, clothing color and silhouette, defining accessories. These\n  should survive stylization. Anything more specific (exact nose shape,\n  pore detail, eye color subtleties) is style-dependent and should NOT\n  be penalised.\n- The REFERENCE asset is the source of truth for what "looks like the\n  source character in this style" should look like. If the reference\n  shows the style well, judge the rendered character against the style\n  example the reference establishes — same level of detail, same kind\n  of line art / brushwork / shading applied to the source\'s character.\n\nFor (d) CHARACTER SWAP:\n- The rendered character should match the REFERENCE (the target person),\n  not the source. Source-vs-stylized mismatch on identity is the whole\n  point of the request.\n- Score `vid_identity` on whether the rendered character matches the\n  reference\'s defining traits (face structure, hair, clothing, signature\n  features).\n\nFor all intents:\n- Within a single source character\'s run of frames, the stylized\n  character must stay CONSISTENT with itself — see `vid_temporal_identity`\n  below. Drift across stylized frames (the rendered face changing from\n  frame to frame within the same person\'s scene) IS a real failure even\n  when no individual frame is "wrong" against the source.\n\nLighting check (compare source frame ↔ stylized output, per pair):\n- Identify the dominant light source(s) in the source: position, direction\n  (cast-shadow direction tells you this), color/temperature, and hardness.\n  Then check whether the stylized output preserves the same setup.\n- Specific failure modes to flag in "vid_issues":\n    * shadow-direction flip\n    * shadows missing where source has them, or invented where source has none\n    * cast shadow shape no longer matches the casting object\n    * specular highlights repositioned on faces, skin, metal, glass\n    * color-temperature shift not requested by the prompt\n    * loss of contact shadows (object looks like it\'s floating)\n    * lighting on the character no longer matches lighting on the background\n- Scoring scale:\n    * 10: shadows, highlights, color temperature, and hardness all\n      consistent with the source across the whole timeline\n    * 7-9: minor drift that a casual viewer wouldn\'t notice\n    * 4-6: noticeable drift — shadows roughly right but wrong shape,\n      highlights shifted, temperature changed enough to read as a different\n      time of day\n    * 0-3: light source has clearly moved, or shadows contradict the\n      source entirely\n- If the prompt explicitly requested a lighting change, score against the\n  request, not the source — but flag any UNREQUESTED secondary drift in\n  "vid_issues".\n\nTemporal consistency — DELTA MATCHING:\nScore the temporal axes by comparing the SOURCE\'s frame-to-frame deltas\nto the STYLIZED output\'s frame-to-frame deltas. The source is the ground\ntruth for "what motion is expected." Reason in three passes:\n\n1. List what changes between SOURCE frames: camera pose, subject pose,\n   subject identity (multi-subject only), background content, lighting.\n2. List what changes between STYLIZED frames: palette, brushwork,\n   subject pose, subject identity, background content.\n3. Penalise MISMATCHES between the two lists. Static→static is OK.\n   Moving→static is drift. A cut in source matched by a cut in stylized\n   is OK. Drift in stylized with no equivalent change in source is the\n   real failure.\n\n- `vid_temporal_style`: does the **rendering style itself** (palette,\n  brushwork, color cohesion, texture treatment) stay consistent across\n  frames?\n  - High score = the same palette / brushwork is applied to every frame.\n  - Low score = palette shifts mid-clip, brush texture flattens, color\n    saturation drifts.\n  - This is **NOT** a measure of scene motion. A static background in\n    the stylized output, when the source background is also static, is\n    correct behaviour and scores HIGH on this axis (the style is fully\n    consistent across frames). The "painted-on-lens" failure only\n    applies when the SOURCE shows background motion that the stylized\n    output erases.\n- `vid_temporal_identity`: per shot-type:\n  - STATIC / DYNAMIC / FAST-ACTION: does the character\'s identity\n    (face, clothing, accessories) stay the same across stylized frames?\n    Mid-clip drift = low score.\n  - MULTI-SUBJECT: score INTRA-character consistency only. Different\n    characters across cuts are NOT identity drift — that\'s the source\'s\n    structure. Within each character\'s run of frames, identity must\n    stay stable; cross-character cuts are scored 10 on this axis if\n    each side preserves its own character\'s identity.\n\nWe do NOT score per-frame flicker in V0 — VLM judgement on whether a\nspecific element "pops" between frames is too noisy without a pixel-level\noptical-flow signal as ground truth. (V1 will combine optical flow with\nthe VLM for the flicker axis.) Treat short-lived element changes as\nidentity / style drift if they persist, and ignore them otherwise.\n\nPer-shot-type rules (apply on top of the delta-matching framing above):\n\n  STATIC source —\n    - Background motion / drift in stylized output = real drift.\n    - Identity drift across stylized frames = real drift.\n    - Watch for the renderer hallucinating motion the source doesn\'t have.\n\n  DYNAMIC source —\n    - Stylized background MUST move with the source. "Painted-on-lens"\n      (background texture locked to the frame instead of to the scene)\n      is the canonical failure here.\n    - Some rendering-style flex is expected as new content enters frame;\n      score temp_style on coherence of palette / brushwork, not on\n      static-pixel similarity.\n\n  MULTI-SUBJECT source —\n    - Identity is judged PER-CHARACTER per cut, not aggregated globally.\n    - Score temp_identity as the WORST intra-character drift, not the\n      cross-character variation.\n    - A clean cut in source matched by a clean cut in stylized is\n      correct; flag in vid_issues only if the stylized output BLENDS\n      across what should be a cut (or vice versa, cuts where the source\n      doesn\'t have one).\n\n  FAST-ACTION source —\n    - Be lenient on per-frame style drift since motion blur naturally\n      varies the rendering.\n    - Stay strict on identity at moments of low motion (the still\n      points within the action).\n\nPer-frame notes (`vid_per_frame_notes`):\n- 0 entries when nothing notable; otherwise short strings calling out\n  drift that CANNOT be explained by natural in-clip motion.\n- Good examples:\n    * "frame 5/8 (t=4.0s): character\'s jacket shifts from red to navy"\n    * "frame 6/8 (t=5.2s): watercolor brush texture flattens on the background"\n    * "frame 4/8 (t=3.2s): facial structure changes — reads as a different person"\n- **Avoid flagging things that can change within a second of natural\n  motion** (blink, mouth open/close, gaze shift, hand position, head turn,\n  small body movement) — those are not drift.\n\nGeneral scoring rules:\n- "vid_pass" is your judgment, not a fixed function of "vid_score"; you may\n  pass a 7.5 with no critical issues, or fail an 8.0 with broken anatomy\n  or mid-clip identity drift.\n- "vid_issues" must be empty when "vid_pass" is true.\n- A perfect 10 is RARE. Reserve it for outputs that have zero observable\n  drift, no temporal artifacts, AND a meaningful refinement is still hard\n  to find.\n\nvid_next_prompt rules:\n- REQUIRED and MUST be a non-empty, NON-TRIVIAL improvement in every\n  response. NEVER reply with "use the same prompt", "no changes needed",\n  or any equivalent. Always identify at least one concrete refinement\n  and fold it into the target description.\n- A self-contained TARGET DESCRIPTION — what the stylized video should\n  look like, written so the renderer can land it in one shot from the\n  source video alone. The renderer will see only the source clip + this\n  prompt; it has NO memory of the previous render. Anything it needs to\n  know (style, content, lighting, identity expectations, framing,\n  temporal stability) must be in the prompt.\n- Phrased as a directive describing the desired result, not a list of\n  complaints or corrections. FORBIDDEN constructions: "fix the…",\n  "stop the drift…", "remove the X artifact", "this render shows…",\n  "the previous attempt…", "correct the…". Instead, describe the\n  correct target directly (e.g. "anime-style render of the source\n  clip with the jacket color held constant across all frames and the\n  background palette stable frame-to-frame").\n- On temporal failures, the required temporal behaviour is part of the\n  target description, not a correction tag. Encode it as a property\n  the rendered clip should have ("character identity locked across\n  cuts", "watercolor palette stable through the dolly move", "no\n  background painted-on-lens drift") — not as something to repair.\n- Avoid model-specific syntax (no negative prompts, no parenthetical\n  weights). Maximum 60 words.\n- When no reference assets are supplied, "vid_style" reflects how well the\n  stylized output matches the intent of the textual prompt. Note the\n  absence of reference assets in "vid_issues" only if it materially blocked\n  judgement.\n';
